#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161780 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" :question/math-academy-id "e-8172" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d054c7daaaeefd4cdf820d49d04d6a0bd544f09e356b859b6291ff7d4588e19.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/98/980726b042df523d4c12352fcde141e8c39701a23d72c2815165517dc8ab6949.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ca/caa7100961d28805820530d11708ac6dee747a75b858494d84fcd4c6e5c5d1fb.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161781 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "f6714653-0cfa-5049-9ae9-f921d46d03bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8173" :question/problem "![](images/da/dacf2240e98ef232fd9b5cd8d12baddb808e6f30c2956d6545370095346c9f22.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the first and second columns of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b9/b90c25fdfb269ffd1c6fe30dc77f9142986f1b72adc23b5e71e2d9a4182795ab.svg) and ![](images/f1/f1313b31d2bce0d0358f5a9255aeb04f13cecfc0d65ae1d1028024ee5a029bc8.svg)\n\nNotice that eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/bf/bf1b9a7dbbca5e1df2e7acb7cf62ef04e35791252bbd8020715c4d7bde23c577.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\\mathbf{e}_2$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8175" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a21020be45202ebc19fdaeb8d3c334775f072ca037d68805e635e824966d7fd2.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/86/860c02625e5bbb5ac0fcb54e0895274ea38d0462d810da582f75351cbe094b36.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492264} {:answer/id #uuid "35d5ccf2-30c9-5cbf-acc9-77d3639bf40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492330} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd90262-baea-5f31-bc98-cf29ad858555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492348} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdd90f8-f543-5147-b91e-eb93bde7c762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492409} {:answer/id #uuid "88c2827e-293f-5e84-bd99-f7c70cbaee50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492264} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a999b2-e6df-50f1-b9e9-4b5772ed9a89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94cf2153-a12b-5a66-9939-44bd440747ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-140829" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e504f2a-130f-56a5-8b73-6535b2866b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492240} {:answer/id #uuid "33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492325} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc11ff8-ed60-5bb7-b2f7-3dc537bc6067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492534} {:answer/id #uuid "d48293d6-ba64-5760-b36c-7134921b5684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492636} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ce4336-3b77-5c48-831a-fa1b69e1b976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492325} :answer-field/id #uuid "020680cf-6e72-5c83-9a57-77d636592189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d11f87e-0592-5806-9319-d12902b8a14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140837" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620276b0-0615-5b71-99fd-2a1e95b3a1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492416} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a7e4db-fff0-5a80-b81b-a973c61d3d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492561} {:answer/id #uuid "b249161d-1326-5a95-ab1f-9495d45cfa68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492572} {:answer/id #uuid "b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492589} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0732c4-d78b-5f63-bac2-1d79627fdaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492589} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" :question/math-academy-id "q-140843" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the second and third columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9b3586-3f9f-5bd3-ad90-e25a93828ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354896} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7867c1-d13a-5967-a996-e401c2653e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355019} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdbffeb-b157-541f-b33a-7f8468f27de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 1