:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1deb190f-aaea-51df-a506-e53c0439123b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94346" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=7.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274efcfe-f893-5105-aa2f-fd156c58b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354997} {:answer/id #uuid "b46ab4aa-3d20-53f3-bd11-038693dbf688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356249} {:answer/id #uuid "d310ec2c-fbb3-5ef7-ba9a-fc3cd195ad9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356505} {:answer/id #uuid "d77a84a6-94a5-5209-b6fd-977d16bd294a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356549} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa9e02e-3860-50bc-b5d0-8cd92dc992c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d310ec2c-fbb3-5ef7-ba9a-fc3cd195ad9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356505} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad9f5dc-8923-584b-b5c4-0c96615d0676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fecab0a-cd00-5e03-af2a-81daa57c4cbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94351" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{2}={v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{{v}_{3}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{{e}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{e}_{2} \\\\  & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{2}$ and ${e}_{3}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the second and third columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{3}}{‖{e}_{3}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{24}{5}}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
